were told the answer to that question, why wasn't there a revolution in Britain, was that Britain was already so liberal that people's desire for reform was already satiated, thanks to the Glorious Reform Act of 1832, and so on. And the Chartists were, okay, well, they were a movement for reform, not for revolution, and your Chartist was a sensible kind of fellow who would rather go home and have tea with his wife than set fire to someone else's house and this kind of thing. And so those are the arguments that were made,
