00:48:42.000 --> 00:49:24.000
And, you know, when President de Gaulle calls out the people France, and actually there are more of them than they are of the people throwing cobblestones at the police, right? It's the same story. But in a way, isn't it even more primal than that? That perhaps we think of 1848 as the revolutionary year, but it's also the year that gives birth perhaps to conservatism as a political force in the way that we would understand it now. Absolutely. That's the complexity of 1848, that revolution and counter-revolution are born twins in the same bed. That's the weird thing about 1848. It's a fascinating thing. But of course, counter-revolution, you know, conservatism is not the same as reaction. There are reactionaries who are saying, you know, we've got to unmake this revolution. We've got to
而且，你知道的，当戴高乐将军号召法国人民时，实际上走出来的人比那些向警察投掷鹅卵石的人还要多，对吧？这是同一个故事。但在某种程度上，难道它不比那更原始吗？也许我们认为1848年是革命之年，但它也是孕育保守主义作为一种政治力量（以我们现在理解的方式）的年份。绝对是的。这就是1848年的复杂性，革命和反革命在同一张床上作为双胞胎诞生。这就是1848年奇特的地方。这是一件引人入胜的事情。但当然，反革命——你知道的，保守主义并不等同于反动。有些反动分子在叫喊，你知道，我们必须废除这场革命。我们必须

